{"abstract":"When designing data science (DS) pipelines, end-users can get overwhelmed by the large and growing set of available data preprocessing and modeling techniques. Intelligent discovery assistants (IDAs) and automated machine learning (AutoML) solutions aim to facilitate end-users by (semi-)automating the process. However, they are expensive to compute and yield limited applicability for a wide range of real-world use cases and application domains. This is due to (a) their need to execute thousands of pipelines to get the optimal one, (b) their limited support of DS tasks, e.g., supervised classification or regression only, and a small, static set of available data preprocessing and ML algorithms; and (c) their restriction to quantifiable evaluation processes and metrics, e.g., tenfold cross-validation using the ROC AUC score for classification. To overcome these limitations, we propose a human-in-the-loop approach for the assisted design of data science pipelines using previously executed pipelines. Based on a user query, i.e., data and a DS task, our framework outputs a ranked list of pipeline candidates from which the user can choose to execute or modify in real time. To recommend pipelines, it first identifies relevant datasets and pipelines utilizing efficient similarity search. It then ranks the candidate pipelines using multi-objective sorting and takes user interactions into account to improve suggestions over time. In our experimental evaluation, the proposed framework significantly outperforms the state-of-the-art IDA tool and achieves similar predictive performance with state-of-the-art long-running AutoML solutions while being real-time, generic to any evaluation processes and DS tasks, and extensible to new operators.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Redyuk, Sergey","Kaoudi, Zoi","Markl, Volker"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776384"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","Technical University of Berlin"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"assisted design of data science pipelines","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00778-024-00835-2","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776384","identifier":[{"id":"85185095712","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2ff36e10-98fa-467b-824b-681dbbd65f72"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-976164591790DD2744E37C15F0BBF066"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00778-024-00835-2","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"german research center for artificial intelligence","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sergey","key":"redyukpau","last":"Redyuk","name":"Redyuk, Sergey","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00778-024-00835-2","issn":["0949877X","10668888"],"issn_org":["10668888"],"issue":"4","journal":"VLDB Journal","pages":"1129-1153","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"VLDB Journal","title_org":"The VLDB Journal","volume":"33"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85185095712"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2ff36e10-98fa-467b-824b-681dbbd65f72"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Assisted design of data science pipelines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776384","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Knowledge graphs (KGs) are being used in many real-world application domains, ranging from search engines to biomedical data analysis. Even if there is a large corpus of KGs available, they are inherently incomplete due to the incompleteness of the sources based on which they were constructed. Knowledge graph embeddings (KGEs) is a very popular technique to complete KGs. However, they are only capable of answering true or false to a given fact. Thus, users need to provide a concrete query or some test data. Unfortunately, such queries or data are not always available. There are cases where users want to discover all (or as many as possible) missing facts from an input KG. Given a KGE model, users should thus provide to the KGE model candidate facts consisting of the complement of the KG. This is infeasible even for small graphs simply due to the size of the complement graph. In this paper, we define the problem of discovering missing facts from a given KGE model and refer to it as fact discovery. We study sampling methods to get candidate facts and then using KGEs to retrieve the most plausible ones. We extensively evaluate different existing sampling methods and provide guidelines on when each one of them is most suitable. We also discuss the challenges and limitations that we encountered when investigating the different techniques. With these insights, we expect to shed light and attract more researchers on this unexplored direction.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Widyadhana Bhagaskoro, Rama","Markl, Volker","Kaoudi, Zoi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776411"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","Technical University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Knowledge Graphs","Knowledge graph embeddings","Sampling methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluation of sampling methods for discovering facts from knowledge graph embeddings","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://openproceedings.org/2024/conf/edbt/paper-144.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776411","identifier":[{"id":"85191007984","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-70cb2e23-5ba7-43c5-abab-9d197f727dfc"],"keyword":["Completeness of knowledge graphs","Fact discovery","Knowledge graph embeddings","Knowledge graphs","Sampling methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3D1AACF738CDD025F4775064978F4A26"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://openproceedings.org/2024/conf/edbt/paper-144.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"german research center for artificial intelligence","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rama","key":"widyadhanabhagaskoropau","last":"Widyadhana Bhagaskoro","name":"Widyadhana Bhagaskoro, Rama","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"664-675","status":"p","title":"EDBT"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-70cb2e23-5ba7-43c5-abab-9d197f727dfc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evaluation of Sampling Methods for Discovering Facts from Knowledge Graph Embeddings","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776411","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"author_count":5,"contributors":["Papadias, Serafeim","Kaoudi, Zoi","Pandey, Varun","Quiane Ruiz, Jorge-Arnulfo","Markl, Volker"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776399"],"event":{"acronym":"ICDE","title":"IEEE International Conference on Data Engineering"},"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","Technical University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Accuracy","Bipartite graph","Estimation","Heuristic algorithms","Image edge detection","Scalability","Throughput"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"counting butterflies in fully dynamic bipartite graph streams","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85200457667","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776399","identifier":[{"id":"85200457667","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4eb2909a-a1d6-4ba3-af0f-561ca739acb6"],"keyword":["Accuracy","Bipartite graph","Estimation","Heuristic algorithms","Image edge detection","Scalability","Throughput"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-31F84105EFB6887C2076CAB52186BA84"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"german research center for artificial intelligence","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"German Research Center for Artificial Intelligence","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Serafeim","key":"papadiaspau","last":"Papadias","name":"Papadias, Serafeim","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Varun","key":"pandeypau","last":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Varun","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jorge-Arnulfo","key":"quianeruizpau","last":"Quiane Ruiz","name":"Quiane Ruiz, Jorge-Arnulfo","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/icde60146.2024.00226","eisbn":["9798350317152"],"isbn":["9798350317169"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2024 IEEE 40th International Conference on Data Engineering (ICDE)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85200457667"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4eb2909a-a1d6-4ba3-af0f-561ca739acb6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Counting Butterflies in Fully Dynamic Bipartite Graph Streams","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776399","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Model parameter management is essential for distributed training of large machine learning (ML) tasks. Some ML tasks are hard to distribute because common approaches to parameter management can be highly inefficient. Advanced parameter management approaches---such as selective replication or dynamic parameter allocation---can improve efficiency, but they typically need to be integrated manually into each task's implementation and they require expensive upfront experimentation to tune correctly. In this work, we explore whether these two problems can be avoided. We first propose a novel intent signaling mechanism that integrates naturally into existing ML stacks and provides the parameter manager with crucial information about parameter accesses. We then describe AdaPM, a fully adaptive, zero-tuning parameter manager based on this mechanism. In contrast to prior parameter managers, our approach decouples how access information is provided (simple) from how and when it is exploited (hard). In our experimental evaluation, AdaPM matched or outperformed state-of-the-art parameter managers out of the box, suggesting that automatic parameter management is possible.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Renz-Wieland, Alexander","Kieslinger, Andreas","Gericke, Robert","Gemulla, Rainer","Kaoudi, Zoi","Markl, Volker"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712541"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Berlin","Universität Mannheim"],"keyword":["Adaptive algorithms","Distributed Training","Dynamic Parameter Allocation","Parameter Management","Selective Replication"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"good intentions adaptive parameter management via intent signaling","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2206.00470","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712541","ids":["itu-97e90803-b139-45fc-bec5-571bdef9b3d4"],"keyword":["Adaptive Algorithms","Distributed Training","Dynamic Parameter Allocation","Parameter Management","Selective Replication"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-6B89FF3FFF0E28B735BA57AC64DDF7DF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.00470.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2206.00470","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"universität mannheim","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universität Mannheim","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alexander","key":"renzwielandpau","last":"Renz-Wieland","name":"Renz-Wieland, Alexander","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"kieslingerpau","last":"Kieslinger","name":"Kieslinger, Andreas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert","key":"gerickepau","last":"Gericke","name":"Gericke, Robert","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rainer","key":"gemullapau","last":"Gemulla","name":"Gemulla, Rainer","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2206.00470","status":"p","title":"CIKM"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-97e90803-b139-45fc-bec5-571bdef9b3d4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Good Intentions: Adaptive Parameter Management via Intent Signaling","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712541","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The large variety of specialized data processing platforms and the increased complexity of data analytics has led to the need for unifying data analytics within a single framework. Such a framework should free users from the burden of (i) choosing the right platform(s) and (ii) gluing code between the different parts of their pipelines. Apache Wayang (Incubating) is the only open-source framework that provides a systematic solution to unified data analytics by integrating multiple heterogeneous data processing platforms. It achieves that by decoupling applications from the underlying platforms and providing an optimizer so that users do not have to specify the platforms on which their pipeline should run. Wayang provides a unified view and processing model, effectively integrating the hodgepodge of heterogeneous platforms into a single framework with increased usability without sacrificing performance and total cost of ownership. In this paper, we present the architecture of Wayang, describe its main components, and give an outlook on future directions.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Beedkar, Kaustubh","Contreras-Rojas, Bertty","Gavriilidis, Haralampos","Kaoudi, Zoi","Markl, Volker","Pardo-Meza, Rodrigo","Quiane Ruiz, Jorge Arnulfo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712521"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, South","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","India"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Indian Institute of Technology Delhi","Technical University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Apache Wayang Architecture","Data Processing Platforms","Heterogeneous Systems Integration","Optimized Data Pipelines","Unified Data Analytics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"apache wayang a unified data analytics framework","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3631504.3631510","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712521","identifier":[{"id":"85176388905","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-58d56a9c-6f26-4d75-8004-63fbb4117538"],"keyword":["Apache Wayang Architecture","Data Processing Platforms","Heterogeneous Systems Integration","Optimized Data Pipelines","Unified Data Analytics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-302081940DB404244F6A63C8392B30EC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"https://sigmodrecord.org/authors/","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106632392/Open_Access_file.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3631504.3631510","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://sigmodrecord.org/publications/sigmodRecord/2309/pdfs/05_Systems_Beedkar.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"in","key":"indian institute of technology delhi","loc":["India","Asia, South"],"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Delhi","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kaustubh","key":"beedkarpau","last":"Beedkar","name":"Beedkar, Kaustubh","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Bertty","key":"contrerasrojaspau","last":"Contreras-Rojas","name":"Contreras-Rojas, Bertty","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Haralampos","key":"gavriilidispau","last":"Gavriilidis","name":"Gavriilidis, Haralampos","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"pardomezapau","last":"Pardo-Meza","name":"Pardo-Meza, Rodrigo","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jorge Arnulfo","key":"quianeruizpau","last":"Quiane Ruiz","name":"Quiane Ruiz, Jorge Arnulfo","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3631504.3631510","issn":["01635808","19435835"],"issn_org":["01635808"],"issue":"3","journal":"SIGMOD Record","pages":"30-35","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"SIGMOD Record","volume":"52"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85176388905"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-58d56a9c-6f26-4d75-8004-63fbb4117538"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Apache Wayang: A Unified Data Analytics Framework","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712521","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Query optimization is crucial for any data management system to achieve good performance. Recent advancements in Machine Learning (ML) have led to several efforts in the database research community that aim at improving query optimization with the help of ML. In particular, many works propose replacing the cost model used during plan enumeration with an ML model. The goal of these works is to learn a regression model from previously executed query plans that estimates the runtime of a given plan. Interestingly, it is well-known that what really matters in query optimization is the order of the query plans and not their actual cost or runtime. We thus take a learning-to-rank approach and propose a novel neural network model architecture that considers a plan in comparison with other equivalent plans that belong to the same query. We use our model architecture together with a loss function that incorporates ranking metrics into the learning process to highlight the learning-to-rank objective.To enable training, we first extract features from query plans by adapting a state-of-the-art deep learning approach so that all features are independent of the input dataset schema. Second, we devise two score functions that map the runtime of plans to scores which are then used as labels. We integrate the trained model into an adapted bottom-up plan enumeration algorithm that finds the best possible execution plan for a given query. We evaluate our approach against two state-of-the-art ML models and the highly tuned cost model of a commercial database and measure the runtime of the plans chosen in each case when executed in the database. We show that our approach achieves up to an order of magnitude better query performance than the comparison models and is able to either match (for short and medium-running queries) or outperform the commercial database (up to 5x for long-running queries).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Behr, Henriette","Markl, Volker","Kaoudi, Zoi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712537"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Cost model","Learning to rank","Query optimization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learn what really matters a learning to rank approach for ml based query optimization","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18420/BTW2023-25","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712537","identifier":[{"id":"85149963280","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-88e1cf46-0c15-4dc0-8248-b282bbb19583"],"keyword":["cost model","learning-to-rank","query optimization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-89AA4BCE86FAFA265F13F93F98446EF4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.gi.de/bitstreams/15b77d75-c38f-42c5-b2fc-8472b284bb3c/download","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18420/BTW2023-25","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henriette","key":"behrpau","last":"Behr","name":"Behr, Henriette","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Volker","key":"marklpau","last":"Markl","name":"Markl, Volker","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Zoi","key":"kaoudipau","last":"Kaoudi","locid":"d5c3a434-36d5-4861-862d-3c5b3e332121","name":"Kaoudi, Zoi","orcid":"0000-0003-4520-5360","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.18420/btw2023-25","edition":"2023","isbn":["9783885797258"],"pages":"535-554","place":"Germany","publisher":"Gesellschaft für Informatik","status":"p","title":"BTW"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85149963280"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-88e1cf46-0c15-4dc0-8248-b282bbb19583"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learn What Really Matters: A Learning-to-Rank Approach for ML-based Query Optimization","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712537","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"A key service in vehicular transportation is routing according to estimated travel times. With the availability of massive volumes of vehicle trajectory data, it has become increasingly feasible to estimate travel times, which are typically modeled as probability distributions in the form of histograms. An earlier study shows that use of a carefully selected, context-dependent subset of available trajectories when estimating a travel-time histogram along a user-specified path can significantly improve the accuracy of the estimates. This selection of trajectories cannot occur in a pre-processing step, but must occur online—it must be integrated into the routing itself. It is then a key challenge to be able to select very efficiently the \"right\" subset of trajectories that offer the best accuracy when the cost of a route is to be assessed. To address this challenge, we propose a solution that applies novel indexing to all available trajectories and that then is capable of selecting the most relevant trajectories and of computing a travel-time distribution based on these trajectories. Specifically, the solution utilizes an in-memory trajectory index and a greedy algorithm to identify and retrieve the relevant trajectories. The paper reports on an extensive empirical study with a large real-world GPS data set that offers insight into the accuracy and efficiency of the proposed solution. The study shows that the proposed online selection of trajectories can be performed efficiently and is able to provide highly accurate travel-time distributions.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Waury, Robert","Jensen, Christian S.","Koide, Satoshi","Ishikawa, Yoshiharu","Xiao, Chuan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-050448"],"event":{"acronym":"EDBT","end":"2019-03-29","place":"Lisbon, pt","start":"2019-03-26","title":"22nd International Conference on Extending Database Technology, EDBT 2019","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["Japan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nagoya University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"indexing trajectories for travel time histogram retrieval","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5441/002/edbt.2019.15","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This research was supported in part by a grant from the Obel Family Foundation and by the DiCyPS project."],"id":"S01-000.00-050448","identifier":[{"id":"85064939071","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-01690e5d-7cf5-49fa-8f25-e7e01d11d735"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-509681198D2223E2F2F373AE1B070D18"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5441/002/edbt.2019.15","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/320710381/EDBT19_paper_75.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Database, Programming and Web Technologies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":